{"item_id": "fresh_N363956", "reference_source": "ruling:N363956", "product": "The item under consideration is described as a tape measure (product number D280179), which is a versatile tool utilized by tradespeople, inspectors, contractors, homeowners, and various consumers for accurate and rapid distance measurement. Its compact, retractable design facilitates the measurement of both short and long lengths, while its portability allows for easy storage in a pocket or toolkit. The instrument features a 5-meter long, flexible polyester ruler tape marked with linear measurements in both inches and centimeters. The remainder of the measuring tape is made of ABS and steel components. It also contains a magnet on the end hook to hold the measuring tape in place.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363956, gold code 9017.80.0000:\nh is a versatile tool utilized by tradespeople, inspectors, contractors, homeowners, and various consumers for accurate and rapid distance measurement. Its compact, retractable design facilitates the measurement of both short and long lengths, while its portability allows for easy storage in a pocket or toolkit. The instrument features a 5-meter long, flexible polyester ruler tape marked with linear measurements in both inches and centimeters. The remainder of the measuring tape is made of ABS and steel components. It also contains a magnet on the end hook to hold the measuring tape in place. The applicable subheading for the tape measure will be 9017.80.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Drawing, marking-out or mathematical calculating instruments (for example, drafting machines, pantographs, protractors, drawing sets, slide rules, disc calculators); instruments for measuring length, for use in the hand (for example, measuring rods and tapes, micrometers, calipers), not specified or included elsewhere in this chapter; parts and accessories thereof: Other instruments.” The general rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any qu", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9017.80.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9017 covers instruments for measuring length for use in the hand (e.g., measuring rods and tapes)", "GRI 6: 9017.80.00.00 other instruments (not drafting, calculating or micrometer/caliper instruments)"], "rationale": "The tape measure is a hand-held instrument for measuring length, which heading 9017 specifically covers. It is not a drafting, calculating or micrometer instrument, so it falls in 9017.80.00.00 (other instruments). CBP classified a retractable tape measure in 9017.80.0000 (G87211, in force).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9017.30", "reason": "micrometers, calipers and gauges; a tape measure is not a gauge"}, {"code": "9017.20", "reason": "drawing, marking-out or mathematical calculating instruments; the tape measures length"}, {"code": "3926.90", "reason": "article of plastics; heading 9017 specifically covers hand measuring tapes"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363968", "reference_source": "ruling:N363968", "product": "The product under consideration is Item 10762 PH Prep Cutting Board System.The item is a two-sided acacia wood (., a tropical wood) cutting board with a removable steel strainer, two removable steelAcacia sppstorage bins with lids, and a removable steel support rack.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363968, gold code 4419.20.9000:\nmparts the main use and the strainer, storage bins, and support rack are secondary to the use of the cutting board.Therefore, the wooden cutting board imparts the essential character of the cutting board system. In your letter, you suggest the cutting board is classifiable under subheading 4419.90.9100, HarmonizedTariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS) which provides for kitchenware of wood other than bamboo ortropical wood.We disagree.The cutting board is made of acacia wood, which is identified as a tropical wood within the Appellation of Certain Tropical Woods in the ENs to Chapter 44.The applicable subheading for Item 10762 PH Prep Cutting Board System will be 4419.20.9000, HTSUS,which provides for Tableware and kitchenware, of wood: Oftropical wood: Other.The general rate of duty will be 3.2 percent ad valorem. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or othercharges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and otherduties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to theclassification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with eitherthe Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisionscovering exceptions to such tariffs.For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated CargoSystems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service.https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remediesThe holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description asidentified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations(CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of theinformation furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate andcomplete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do notconform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs andBorder Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2.Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodicverification by CBP.This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border ProtectionRegulations (19 C.F.R. 177).A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documentsfiled at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contactNational Import Specialist Laurel Duvall at .laurel.duvall@cbp.dhs.gov Sincerely, (for)James P. ForkanDirectorNational Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4419.90.91.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1/2(b): prima facie classifiable in 4419 (kitchenware of wood) and 7323 (household articles of steel)", "GRI 3(b): composite good / set for food prep; the acacia wood cutting board, the principal work surface, gives the essential character; the steel strainer, bins and rack are ancillary", "GRI 6: 4419.90 other (not bamboo); 4419.90.91.00 other than forks and spoons"], "rationale": "The cutting board system combines an acacia wood board with removable steel strainer, bins and rack, so it is a composite good or set classified under GRI 3(b). The wood cutting board is the central work surface and gives the essential character, as CBP found for a wood board with an ancillary component in N010808 (in force). As kitchenware of non-bamboo wood other than forks and spoons, it falls in 4419.90.91.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7323.93.00", "reason": "steel strainer, bins and rack play a secondary role to the wood cutting surface"}, {"code": "4419.1x", "reason": "those lines are for bamboo; board is acacia wood"}, {"code": "4419.90.11.00", "reason": "not forks or spoons"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363991", "reference_source": "ruling:N363991", "product": "The “Outdoor Mounting Kit” is a wood and metal ground support anchor that will function as a pedestal base for the Little Free Library (sold separately). The kit will consist of (1) a pressure-treated pine wood upper mounting plate that will attach to the post, (2) a decorative pressure-treated pine wood post, (3) a black powder-coated galvanized steel helical ground anchor that will be screwed directly into the soil, and (4) assembly hardware. The mounting plate will have a laser engraved phrase that states, “Take a book & Leave a book.” The mounting plate measures approximately 28cm in length, 19cm in width, and 7.6cm in height. The wooden post will have a flat full face and sloped recessed panel inserts on each side. The wooden post measures approximately 800mm in length and 89mm in width. The tapered anchor measures approximately 7cm in diameter and 68.5cm in length. The kit will mount to the Little Free Library and is intended to be installed above ground in a garden or in front of a private residence where members of the community may freely leave and take books. In the condition at the time of importation into the United States, the kit will be packaged together and offered for retail sale as one unit. In view of the facts provided, the upper plate of the “Outdoor Mounting Kit” will attach to the Little Free Library’s wooden storage box, the wooden post, the metal", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363991, gold code 9403.91.0080:\nplate and post]; (b) consists of products or articles put together to meet a particular need or carry out a specific activity [support]; and (c) are put up in a manner suitable for sale directly to end users without repacking, therefore, the subject merchandise will be considered a set for customs purposes. Additionally, the decorative and laser engraved wood components are the indispensable attribute that strongly marks or serves to distinguish the structure, core or condition of the article. The essential character of the “Outdoor Mounting Kit” as a whole is imparted by the wood components. The applicable subheading for the subject merchandise will be 9403.91.0080, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Other furniture and parts thereof: Parts: Of wood: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Dharmendra Lilia at dharmendra.lilia@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9403.91.00.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the Little Free Library book box is furniture of heading 9403 (CBP N350230 classified an outdoor literature storage box in 9403.60); the mounting kit is dedicated to it and serves as its pedestal base", "Chapter 94 note / heading 9403 covers parts of furniture; kit is solely designed to mount to the Little Free Library", "GRI 3(b): wood plate and post give the essential character of the kit over the steel anchor and hardware", "GRI 6: 9403.91.00 parts of furniture of wood; 9403.91.00.80 other"], "rationale": "The Outdoor Mounting Kit is a pedestal base designed solely to support the Little Free Library book box, which CBP treats as furniture of heading 9403 (N350230, in force). As a dedicated component that attaches to and supports that furniture, it is a part of furniture. The wood mounting plate and post form the bulk of the kit and give it its essential character under GRI 3(b), so it falls in 9403.91.00.80, parts of furniture of wood, other.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "4421.99.98", "reason": "other articles of wood; the kit is a dedicated part (base) of furniture of 9403, which is more specific"}, {"code": "9403.99", "reason": "parts of other materials; wood components predominate over the steel anchor"}, {"code": "7308.90 / 7326.90", "reason": "steel anchor does not give essential character"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364000", "reference_source": "ruling:N364000", "product": "The subject merchandise, “Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces,” is a Quorn-branded line of meat-free products made from the principle ingredient, mycoprotein, a meat free protein. The product will be imported frozen, in Quorn-branded retail packaging weighing 28.2 ounces (800 grams). Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces is said to contain mycoprotein paste, natural flavoring, yeast extract, onion powder, sage, sugar, salt, sunflower oil and free range egg white powder.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364000, gold code 2106.90.9991:\nested a tariff classification ruling. An ingredients breakdown and photographs of the retail packaging accompanied your inquiry. The subject merchandise, “Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces,” is a Quorn-branded line of meat-free products made from the principle ingredient, mycoprotein, a meat free protein. The product will be imported frozen, in Quorn-branded retail packaging weighing 28.2 ounces (800 grams). Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces is said to contain mycoprotein paste, natural flavoring, yeast extract, onion powder, sage, sugar, salt, sunflower oil and free range egg white powder. The applicable subheading for the product, “Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces,” will be 2106.90.9991, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Food preparations not elsewhere specified or included: Other: Other: Other: Frozen.” The general rate of duty will be 6.4 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This merchandise is subject to The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which is regulated by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information on the Bioterrorism Act can be obtained by calling FDA at 301-575-0156, or at the Web site www.fda.gov/oc/bioterrorism/bioact.html. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry do", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "2106.90.99.91", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 2106 covers food preparations not elsewhere specified or included; mycoprotein imitation meat is not a meat preparation of chapter 16 and is not specified elsewhere", "GRI 6: 2106.90 other; not dairy/sugar quota lines, not beverages; residual other food preparations; frozen", "GRI 6 statistical: 2026 revision moved the former frozen line 2106.90.99.95 to 2106.90.99.91 (confirmed with hts_revision_diff)"], "rationale": "Quorn Meatless Diced ChiQin Pieces are a mycoprotein-based imitation meat, a food preparation not elsewhere specified in heading 2106. CBP has classified the same Quorn ChiQin Pieces in 2106.90.9995 (N360739, in force). That frozen statistical line was renumbered in the current revision, and the frozen line is now 2106.90.99.91.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "1602.32", "reason": "contains no meat; chapter 16 covers preparations of meat"}, {"code": "2106.90.99.90", "reason": "product is frozen, not canned"}, {"code": "2106.90.99.95", "reason": "removed in 2026 revision; frozen line now 2106.90.99.91"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364356", "reference_source": "ruling:N364356", "product": "The products in question are the Silent-Aire Ballard Air Handling Units with Direct Evaporative Cooling (Ballard AHU DEC), items numbers 127-003229-009, 127-003924-001, and SA-500PN-ICVD. These industrial units rely on an integrated, direct evaporative cooling section built into the AHU and are designed for air-cooling applications in data centers and other large-scale facilities. These machines operate by drawing air through wetted evaporative media. As air passes through the media, water evaporates into the airstream, reducing air temperature. All units incorporate motor-driven fans, fixed evaporative cooling media, water distribution and drainage systems, and integrated controls to regulate airflow, waterflow, and operational parameters. The Ballard AHU DEC units do not employ refrigeration, compressed vapor cycles, or humidity-regulating devices characteristic of air conditioning machines. While humidity levels are affected, this is a secondary effect of the system and not the machines’ core function; any humidity functions occur as an indirect result of the cooling function. The units are not specifically designed to change the humidity in the environment. While the Ballard AHU DEC air handling units also filter and distribute the air, as noted their principal function is to cool the space through direct evaporative cooling.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364356, gold code 8479.60.0000:\nhe Ballard AHU DEC units do not employ refrigeration, compressed vapor cycles, or humidity-regulating devices characteristic of air conditioning machines. While humidity levels are affected, this is a secondary effect of the system and not the machines’ core function; any humidity functions occur as an indirect result of the cooling function. The units are not specifically designed to change the humidity in the environment. While the Ballard AHU DEC air handling units also filter and distribute the air, as noted their principal function is to cool the space through direct evaporative cooling. The applicable subheading for the Silent-Aire Ballard Air Handling Units with Direct Evaporative Cooling will be 8479.60.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for Machines and mechanical appliances having individual functions, not specified or included elsewhere in this chapter; parts thereof: Evaporative air coolers. The general rate of duty will be 2.8 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Arthur Purcell at arthur.purcell@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commo", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8479.60.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8415 requires a motor-driven fan and elements for changing temperature and humidity; the units do not use refrigeration and are not designed to regulate humidity, so they are not air conditioning machines", "Section XVI note 3: principal function is cooling by direct evaporation; filtering and air distribution are secondary", "GRI 1: subheading 8479.60 specifically names evaporative air coolers; 8479.60.00.00 is the statistical line"], "rationale": "The Ballard AHU DEC units cool air by passing it through wetted evaporative media and use no refrigeration or humidity-regulating devices, so they fall outside heading 8415 air conditioning machines. Their principal function under Section XVI note 3 is evaporative cooling, which subheading 8479.60 names eo nomine. No on-point CBP ruling was found in the corpus, so the classification rests on the heading and subheading text.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8415.82", "reason": "not an air conditioning machine: no refrigeration and humidity change is only incidental"}, {"code": "8414.59", "reason": "fans are only one component; principal function is evaporative cooling"}, {"code": "8421.39", "reason": "air filtration is secondary to cooling"}, {"code": "8479.89", "reason": "8479.60 specifically names evaporative air coolers"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364408", "reference_source": "ruling:N364408", "product": "The product at issue is a tire inflator, model SP00006. This device features an air hose assembly, a digital LCD display for pressure readings, and a valve housed in a pistol-grip body design (main body and trigger) constructed of plastic material. Once the power button is activated, the user can monitor tire pressure and based on the display reading, operate the valve to inflate or deflate the tire as needed. The tire inflator is packaged with batteries from China.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364408, gold code 8481.80.5090:\n operated piston), an internal combustion motor, or any other motor…” Based on the submitted information, the digital tire inflator does not meet the terms of the heading, as it does not include a motor. As a result, classification in heading 8467, HTSUS, is precluded. Similar to the water spray valves in New York Ruling Letters N324135, dated March 1, 2022, and N337994, dated March 4, 2024, the tire inflator functions essentially as a hand-operated valve that regulates the inflation and deflation process. Valves and similar appliances are specifically provided for within heading 8481, HTSUS. The applicable subheading for the tire inflator will be 8481.80.5090, HTSUS, which provides for Taps, cocks, valves and similar appliances, for pipes, boiler shells, tanks, vats or the like, including pressure-reducing valves and thermostatically controlled valves; parts thereof: Other appliances: Hand operated: Of other materials: Other. The general rate of duty will be 3 percent ad valorem. With response to origin, the final assembly of the tire inflator occurs in Malaysia using components primarily from China and a pistol-grip body from Malaysia. The final assembly begins by installing an O-ring onto a valve core from China. In the next step, a spring is installed into the valve seat of a trigger from Malaysia. This step is followed by the installation of a valve core and a seat plug into the valve seat. The process continues by inserting an O-ring, a steel ball and a spring into a valve cap hole. The trigger assembly (a valve cap from Malaysia, the trigger and a pivot pin) is then c onnected to a main body from Malaysia. In the next step, an air inlet fitting and air hose assembly from China are screwed into the main body. Finally, a gauge assembly (produced in Malaysia using components from Malaysia and China) is mounted on the main body and screwed in place. Once finished, the device is tested, inspected and packaged with batteries from China. When determining the country of origin for purposes of applying current trade remedies under Section 301 and additional duties, the substantial transformation analysis is applicable. See, e.g., Headquarters Ruling Letter (HQ) H301619, dated November 6, 2018. The test for determining whether a substantial transformation will occur is whether an article emerges from a process with a new name, character, or use different from that possessed by the article prior to processing. See Texas Instruments Inc. v. United States, 681 F.2d 778 (C.C.P.A. 1982). This determination is based on the totality of the evidence. See National Hand Tool Corp. v. United States, 16 C.I.T. 308 (1992), aff’d, 989 F.2d 1201 (Fed. Cir. 1993). When determining the country of origin of valves, CBP has relied on the origin of the valve body, the essential component of a valve, as the process of assembling a valve is rather simple. Components are typically assembled into a valve body by inserting, pressing, and screwing components to each other. See New York Rulings N339513, dated May 6, 2024, N338713, dated April 3, 2024, N325765, dated May 26, 2022, and N322652, dated December 2, 2021. Therefore, as the processes described above do not result in a substantial transformation, we rely on the origin of the valve body, which consists of the trigger and the main body, to determine the origin of the product. Therefore, as the origin of the valve body is Malaysia, the origin of the tire inflator is Malaysia. The holding set forth above applie", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8481.80.50.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8481 covers taps, cocks, valves and similar appliances for pipes, tanks or the like; the inflator is a hand-operated valve controlling air flow into/out of a tire", "GRI 3(b): the valve mechanism, not the digital gauge, gives the essential character (gauge is an aid to operating the valve)", "GRI 6: 8481.80 other appliances; hand operated; 8481.80.50 of other materials (plastic body); 8481.80.50.90 other"], "rationale": "The tire inflator is a hand-operated valve in a plastic pistol-grip body that lets the user inflate or deflate a tire while an LCD gauge shows the pressure. CBP classified tire inflators with gauge and gun grip as hand-operated valves of other materials in 8481.80.5090 (N054119, in force). The digital gauge is subsidiary to the valve function, and the plastic body points to the 'other materials' line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9026.20.80", "reason": "pressure gauge function is subsidiary to the valve function that controls inflation/deflation"}, {"code": "8414.80", "reason": "device has no compressor or pump; it only controls air from an external source"}, {"code": "8481.80.30", "reason": "body is plastic, not iron or steel"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364413", "reference_source": "ruling:N364413", "product": "The merchandise under consideration consists of auxiliary motorcycle footpeg kits imported from Norway. Each kit consists of two footpegs with stainless steel mounting hardware, and is designed for installation on motorcycles. According to the information provided, a complete kit weighs approximately 1,700 grams, of which approximately 1,550 grams consists of aluminum alloy and the remaining approximately 150 grams consists of stainless steel. The auxiliary footpegs are model-specific and are installed in addition to the motorcycle’s original equipment footpegs, which remain in place. The subject footpegs mount directly to the motorcycle frame and provide the rider with an additional foot position during operation.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364413, gold code 8714.10.0050:\nminum alloy EN AW-6082 T6. According to the information provided, a complete kit weighs approximately 1,700 grams, of which approximately 1,550 grams consists of aluminum alloy and the remaining approximately 150 grams consists of stainless steel. The auxiliary footpegs are model-specific and are installed in addition to the motorcycle’s original equipment footpegs, which remain in place. The subject footpegs mount directly to the motorcycle frame and provide the rider with an additional foot position during operation. The footpegs are designed solely for use with motorcycles of heading 8711. The applicable subheading for the auxiliary motorcycle footpeg kits will be 8714.10.0050, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Parts and accessories of vehicles of headings 8711 to 8713: Of motorcycles (including mopeds): Other.” The general rate of duty will be Free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Matthew Sullivan at matthew.sullivan@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8714.10.00.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVII note 2: footpegs are not parts of general use (Section XV note 2) nor otherwise excluded; the stainless mounting hardware is packaged with the kit and follows it", "Section XVII note 3: footpegs are model-specific and suitable solely or principally for motorcycles of heading 8711", "GRI 1: heading 8714 covers parts and accessories of vehicles of headings 8711 to 8713; 8714.10 of motorcycles", "GRI 6: 8714.10.00.50 other (not saddles/seats or wheels)"], "rationale": "The auxiliary footpegs are model-specific accessories that mount to the motorcycle frame, so under Section XVII note 3 they are suitable solely or principally for motorcycles, and no exclusion in Section XVII note 2 applies. Heading 8714 covers parts and accessories of motorcycles. CBP classified a motorcycle Highway Peg Kit as motorcycle parts and accessories (G88285, under the older 8714.19.0060 numbering), and current rulings use 8714.10.0050 (N340630). The kit falls in 8714.10.00.50.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7616.99.51", "reason": "article of aluminum; Section XVII note 3 places dedicated motorcycle accessories in 8714"}, {"code": "8714.10.00.10", "reason": "not saddles or seats"}, {"code": "8714.19.00.60", "reason": "obsolete numbering used in older rulings; current line is 8714.10.00.50"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364446", "reference_source": "ruling:N364446", "product": "The item under consideration is described as a smart transmitter, which is a specialized, reusable medical device designed exclusively for use with the Eversense365 Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) system. As an integral component of this system, the transmitter is worn externally over an implanted glucose sensor to provide power and facilitate the wireless transmission of real-time glucose data, alerts, and trends to a compatible mobile application. Engineered for continuous wear, the device features an oval-shaped polycarbonate enclosure measuring approximately 48.0mm by 37.7mm by 9.2mm and weighing 14.0 grams. It is powered by an integrated, rechargeable lithium-polymer battery with an operational life of approximately 12 months, and it maintains connectivity with the mobile app at distances up to 24.9 feet under normal operating conditions. At the time of importation, these units are shipped separately from the implantable sensors in trays of 20, intended for subsequent integration into the final commercial packaging. Once combined with the sensor, the transmitter serves as a critical element of the Eversense365 system, which is indicated for the continuous measurement of glucose levels in adults with diabetes for up to one year.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364446, gold code 9027.90.5695:\nersense365 CGM System and has no independent commercial application. In our opinion, the Eversense365 functions similarly to the CGM system addressed in New York Ruling Letter N354553, dated October 25, 2025, in which CBP classified the device in heading 9027, HTSUS. According to Note 2(b) to Chapter 90, HTSUS, parts and accessories suitable for use solely or principally with a particular instrument or apparatus are classified with that instrument or apparatus. As an integral part of the Eversense365 CGM System, the smart transmitter is precluded from classification under heading 9018, HTSUS. The applicable subheading for the smart transmitter will be 9027.90.5695, HTSUS, which provides for “Instruments and apparatus for physical or chemical analysis (for example, polarimeters, refractometers, spectrometers, gas or smoke analysis apparatus); instruments and apparatus for measuring or checking viscosity, porosity, expansion, surface tension or the like; instruments and apparatus for measuring or checking quantities of heat, sound or light (including exposure meters); microtomes; parts and accessories thereof: Microtomes; parts and accessories: Parts and accessories: Of electrical instruments and apparatus: Other: Of instruments and apparatus of subheading 9027.20, 9027.30, 9027.50, 9027.81 or 9027.89: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. In your letter, you also requested consideration of a secondary classification for the subject smart transmitter under 9817.00.96, HTSUS, which applies to articles and parts of articles specifically designed or adapted for the use or benefit of the blind or other physically or mentally handicapped persons. Chapter 98, Subchapter XVII, U.S. Note 4(a), HTSUS, defines the term blind or other physically or mentally handicapped persons as including “any person suffering from a permanent or chronic physical or mental impairment which substantially limits one or more major life activities, such as caring for oneself, performing manual tasks, walking, seeing, hearing, speaking, breathing, learning, or working.” We note that in Headquarters Ruling (HQ) 561020, dated October 14, 1998, U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) held that people with diabetes suffer from a permanent or physical impairment within the meaning of U.S. Note 4(a) to Chapter 98 of the HTSUS. In HQ 562869, dated December 23, 2003, CBP held that a pump designed for individuals suffering from diabetes or glucose control problems was an article specifically designed or adapted for the handicapped and is properly classified under 9817.00.96, HTSUS, for secondary classification purposes. In New York Ruling Letter N292225, dated December 18, 2017, CBP held that Insulet’s Insulin Delivery Omnipod was classified under 9817.00.96 for secondary classification purposes. Additionally, as discussed in HQ 964169, dated June 26, 2001, “people with diabetes are limited in their ability to perform a broad range of jobs because they must be able to monitor their blood sugar, inject insulin if prescribed, and have work restrictions due to excessive urination, possible nausea, dizziness and fainting. This interferes with working, a major life activity. Therefore, persons with diabetes suffer from a permanent or chronic physical impairment which substantially limits a major life activity and therefore, are considered physically handicapped persons under U.S. Note 4(a).” The smart transmitter is designed exclusively for use with the Eversense365 CGM Sys", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9027.90.56.95", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: complete CGM system is an instrument for chemical analysis (glucose measurement) of heading 9027, subheading 9027.89", "Chapter 90 note 2(b): the transmitter is designed exclusively for and is an integral component of the Eversense365 CGM system, so it is classified as a part of that instrument; it is not a standalone good of another heading under note 2(a) since it also powers and operates the sensor", "GRI 6: 9027.90 parts and accessories; 9027.90.56 of instruments of 9027.89; 9027.90.56.95 other"], "rationale": "The smart transmitter is designed exclusively for the Eversense365 CGM system, powering the implanted sensor and relaying glucose data to the app. Under chapter 90 note 2(b) it is classified with the instrument it serves, a glucose-measuring instrument of heading 9027. CBP has classified CGM sensors, which N354553 (in force) describes as part of a system that includes the transmitter, as parts and accessories in 9027.90.5695. The transmitter follows the same line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8517.62.00", "reason": "the wireless link is one of several functions; the transmitter also powers the implanted sensor and is designed solely for the CGM system (chapter 90 note 2(b)); arguable under note 2(a)"}, {"code": "9018.19.95.60", "reason": "CBP treats CGM systems as instruments for chemical analysis of 9027, not electro-diagnostic apparatus"}, {"code": "9027.89.45", "reason": "the transmitter alone is not a complete measuring instrument"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364644", "reference_source": "ruling:N364644", "product": "The items in question are Ceme-Tube® single-use cylindrical tube molds for pouring concrete piers, footings, columns, and bollards. These cast-in-place forms are 4 feet in length but can be stacked to form concrete piers from 12 feet to 20 feet. The forms are made from partially recycled HDPE plastic and available in 8\", 10\", 12\", 18\", 24\", and 30\" diameters.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364644, gold code 8480.60.0090:\n8480.60.0090 Scott Stevens Greif 366 Greif Parkway Delaware, OH 43015 RE: The tariff classification of cement forming molds from Canada Dear Mr. Stevens: In your letter dated August 27, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. The items in question are Ceme-Tube® single-use cylindrical tube molds for pouring concrete piers, footings, columns, and bollards. These cast-in-place forms are 4 feet in length but can be stacked to form concrete piers from 12 feet to 20 feet. The forms are made from partially recycled HDPE plastic and available in 8\", 10\", 12\", 18\", 24\", and 30\" diameters. The applicable subheading for the Ceme-Tube® cement forming molds will be 8480.60.0090, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for molds for mineral materials, other than injection or compression type. The rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Arthur Purcell at arthur.purcell@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8480.60.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8480 covers molds for mineral materials; concrete is a mineral material and the tube shapes poured concrete into piers/columns", "GRI 1: heading 8480 applies regardless of the mold's material (plastic), and 8480 is more specific than 3926 other articles of plastics", "GRI 6: 8480.60.00 molds for mineral materials; 8480.60.00.90 other types (not injection or compression)"], "rationale": "The Ceme-Tube forms are cylindrical molds into which wet concrete is poured to form piers, footings and columns, which makes them molds for mineral materials of heading 8480. CBP consistently classifies concrete formwork in 8480.60.0090 (N353614, in force; R03115). Being single-use and made of HDPE does not remove them from the molds provision, and as gravity-pour forms they are 'other types', not injection or compression molds.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3926.90.99", "reason": "heading 8480 specifically provides for molds for mineral materials"}, {"code": "3917.21-3917.32", "reason": "not tubes for conveying fluids; function is molding concrete"}, {"code": "8480.60.00.10", "reason": "not an injection or compression mold"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N364732", "reference_source": "ruling:N364732", "product": "PA6-imide is a polyamide based thermoplastic monofilament. It is an extruded monofilament having a solid, circular cross-section with a diameter of 1.75 millimeters. Documentation notes that the monofilament contains no reinforcements or filler. The monofilament will be imported on spools. The spools serve solely to hold and dispense the monofilament and contain no coding or identification circuitry. The principal use of the monofilament is feedstock for material extrusion additive manufacturing.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N364732, gold code 3916.90.3000:\nar Mr. Henry: In your letter dated August 31, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. PA6-imide is a polyamide based thermoplastic monofilament. It is an extruded monofilament having a solid, circular cross-section with a diameter of 1.75 millimeters. Documentation notes that the monofilament contains no reinforcements or filler. The monofilament will be imported on spools. The spools serve solely to hold and dispense the monofilament and contain no coding or identification circuitry. The principal use of the monofilament is feedstock for material extrusion additive manufacturing. The applicable subheading for the polyamide based thermoplastic monofilament will be 3916.90.3000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for monofilament of which any cross-sectional dimension exceeds 1 mm, rods, sticks and profile shapes, whether or not surface-worked but not otherwise worked, of plastics: of other plastics: other: other: monofilament. The general rate of duty will be 6.5 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Christina Allen at julie.c.allen@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3916.90.30.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3916 covers monofilament of plastics of which any cross-sectional dimension exceeds 1 mm; 1.75 mm diameter qualifies", "Section XI / chapter 54 exclude monofilament exceeding 1 mm cross-section, so not synthetic filament yarn of 5404", "GRI 5(b): spools are ordinary packing/holding devices, classified with the filament", "GRI 6: 3916.90 of other plastics (polyamide); other; other (not racket strings); 3916.90.30.00 monofilament"], "rationale": "The PA6-imide product is an extruded polyamide monofilament 1.75 mm in diameter, which exceeds 1 mm, so heading 3916 covers it rather than the textile monofilament provisions of chapter 54. The spools are ordinary holding devices. CBP classified 3D-printing filament of another plastic (PEEK) as monofilament in 3916.90.3000 (N356246, in force). The polyamide filament falls in 3916.90.30.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "5404.11 / 5404.19", "reason": "synthetic monofilament of 67 decitex or more with cross-section not exceeding 1 mm; this filament is 1.75 mm"}, {"code": "3916.90.50.00", "reason": "covers rods, sticks and profile shapes; the article is a monofilament"}, {"code": "3916.90.20.00", "reason": "not racket strings"}, {"code": "3908.10", "reason": "not a primary form; it is an extruded monofilament"}]}}
